# Foundations area: groups 11 and 12
ML = [
dict(id="perceptron", g=11, title="The perceptron", tag="a neuron is a weighted vote, and a vote is a line",
 intro="The smallest possible learner: one neuron, one line. Everything in deep learning is this unit, repeated and stacked — and the one thing it cannot do, XOR, is the reason networks have hidden layers at all.",
 problem="You have labelled points and want a rule that classifies a new one. The rule has to be <em>learned</em> from the data, not written by hand, and it has to be something a machine can adjust.",
 solves="A weighted sum plus a threshold draws a line through the input space, and a one-line update rule moves that line toward every mistake until none remain — guaranteed, as long as a separating line exists.",
 method="Compute <b>s = w·x + b</b> and fire if s &gt; 0. On a mistake with true label t, set <b>w ← w + t·x</b> and <b>b ← b + t</b>: adding a point to w pulls the line toward it, subtracting pushes it away. A second layer of neurons <b>bends the space</b> so that XOR, impossible for one line, becomes separable.",
 rel=[("forward-pass","the same neuron, with real numbers, stacked into layers"),("backprop","the update rule generalised: gradients instead of ±x"),("self-attention","the same matmul-and-bend inside a transformer")],
 trade=[("Only linear boundaries","one neuron is one line; XOR needs a hidden layer"),("Converges to a line, not the best line","the margin is whatever the update order produced — logistic regression and SVMs fix this"),("No notion of confidence","a step output is ±1; replacing it with a sigmoid gives a probability, which is what makes a loss differentiable")],
 say="A neuron draws a line. A layer bends the space so the next line works. Depth is repeated bending."),

dict(id="forward-pass", g=11, title="The forward pass", tag="weighted sums and bends, layer by layer",
 intro="What a network actually computes when you call it. Two inputs, two hidden neurons, one output, every number by hand — and the same two lines are exactly what a trillion-parameter model does at scale.",
 problem="A single neuron can only draw a line. To represent anything curved, the outputs of neurons have to become the inputs of other neurons — and something has to stop the stack from collapsing back into one line.",
 solves="Alternating a linear map (matmul + bias) with a nonlinearity (ReLU, sigmoid) gives a composition that can represent essentially any function — and whose every intermediate value can be stored for the backward pass.",
 method="Layer 1: <b>z = W₁x + b₁</b>, then <b>h = ReLU(z)</b>. Layer 2: <b>u = w₂·h + b₂</b>, then <b>ŷ = σ(u)</b>, a probability. The loss <b>L = −log ŷ</b> (cross-entropy, y = 1) turns <em>how wrong</em> into one number. With x = (1.0, 0.5): z = (0.5, 1.0), h = (0.5, 1.0), u = 0, ŷ = 0.5, L = 0.693.",
 rel=[("perceptron","one of these neurons, learning on its own"),("activation-functions","the bend, and why ReLU"),("backprop","the same numbers, run backwards"),("self-attention","a transformer layer is this plus attention"),("prefill-vs-decode","inference is nothing but forward passes")],
 trade=[("Every intermediate is kept for training","z, h, u, ŷ — activation memory, the reason training needs several times the memory of inference"),("Cost is dominated by matmul","batch × d_in × d_out per layer — which is what GPUs are built for"),("The bend is not optional","without it depth buys nothing: W₂(W₁x) is one linear map")],
 say="Forward = matmul, bend, matmul, bend. Store everything — backward is about to need it."),

dict(id="activation-functions", g=11, title="Activation functions", tag="the bend, and the slope backprop multiplies by",
 intro="Step, sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, GELU, softmax. Each one is a different answer to the same two questions: how does it bend the signal going forward, and what does it multiply the gradient by going back? The second question decided the history of deep learning.",
 problem="A network needs a nonlinearity between layers or depth means nothing — but backprop multiplies one slope per layer, so a function whose slope is small or zero starves every layer before it of gradient.",
 solves="ReLU and its smooth relatives keep the slope at 1 wherever the unit is active, so gradients survive depth; sigmoid and softmax are kept for the output, where a bounded probability is the point.",
 method="<b>Sigmoid</b> squashes to (0, 1), slope at most 0.25. <b>tanh</b> to (−1, 1), slope at most 1; both saturate. <b>ReLU</b> = max(0, z): slope 1 when on, 0 when off. <b>GELU/SiLU</b> smooth the corner, used in transformers. <b>Softmax</b> turns a vector of logits into a distribution; with cross-entropy its gradient at the logits is p − onehot(y).",
 rel=[("forward-pass","where the bend sits in the computation"),("backprop","why the slope is what matters"),("perceptron","the step function, and why it cannot be trained"),("self-attention","softmax is also what attention uses to turn scores into weights")],
 trade=[("Saturating functions vanish","ten sigmoid layers multiply a gradient by ~10⁻⁷; early layers never learn"),("ReLU units can die","z < 0 for every input means slope 0 forever; leaky ReLU, GELU and a sane learning rate prevent it"),("The output is chosen by the loss","sigmoid for one probability, softmax for a distribution, identity for a number — not by taste")],
 say="Pick the hidden activation for its slope and the output activation for its range. ReLU/GELU inside, softmax at the end — that is every LLM."),

dict(id="backprop", g=12, title="Backpropagation", tag="the chain rule, run backwards",
 intro="How a network finds out which way each of its weights should move. It is the chain rule applied once per node, in reverse, reusing stored forward values — nothing more exotic than that, and worth doing by hand exactly once.",
 problem="Lowering the loss means knowing ∂L/∂w for every weight, and a weight deep in the network affects L only through everything after it. Deriving each gradient separately would repeat the same work millions of times.",
 solves="One backward sweep. The gradient at the output is just the error ŷ − y; each layer multiplies the incoming signal by its local derivative and hands it back. Every ∂L/∂w falls out as <em>upstream signal × the input that weight multiplies</em>.",
 method="Start at the end: <b>∂L/∂u = ŷ − y = −0.5</b>. Output weights: <b>∂L/∂w₂ = −0.5·h = (−0.25, −0.5)</b>. Back through the weights: <b>∂L/∂h = −0.5·w₂ = (−0.2, 0.4)</b>. Through ReLU: multiply by 1 where z &gt; 0. First-layer weights: <b>∂L/∂W₁ = ∂L/∂z ⊗ x</b>. Eight gradients, one pass.",
 rel=[("forward-pass","the values this reuses"),("autodiff","the same sweep, done by the framework"),("optimizers","what the gradients are for"),("flashattention","recompute in backward instead of storing — the same trade")],
 trade=[("About twice the forward cost","two matmuls per layer going back — one for ∂W, one for ∂h — so training ≈ 3× forward FLOPs"),("Needs the forward activations","or recomputes them: activation checkpointing"),("Dead units learn nothing","a ReLU that was off passes zero gradient, so its weights get no signal from that example"),("Signals shrink or blow up with depth","vanishing and exploding gradients — the reason residual connections and normalisation exist")],
 say="Gradient of a weight = upstream signal × the input it multiplies. Going backwards through a weight means multiplying by that weight."),

dict(id="autodiff", g=12, title="Automatic differentiation", tag="what loss.backward() actually does",
 intro="Nobody writes backprop by hand. The framework records the forward computation as a graph, and one call walks it backwards. Knowing what it records — and what it has to keep in memory — is the difference between using PyTorch and understanding it.",
 problem="A modern model is thousands of operations whose derivatives would be impossible to derive and maintain by hand, and the gradient is needed with respect to every one of billions of parameters.",
 solves="Each operation knows only its own local derivative. Recording operations as they run (define-by-run) and visiting them in reverse order assembles the full gradient from local pieces — one sweep for all parameters, at about twice the cost of the forward pass.",
 method="Forward: every op appends a node holding its <b>grad_fn</b> and saves the tensors its derivative needs. <code>loss.backward()</code> seeds 1 at the loss and visits nodes in reverse topological order; each multiplies the incoming gradient by its local derivative (a vector–Jacobian product) and passes the result to its inputs. Whatever reaches a leaf accumulates into <b>.grad</b>.",
 rel=[("backprop","the same arithmetic, by hand"),("optimizers","the next line: optimizer.step()"),("forward-pass","what the recorded graph computes"),("agent-context-growth","another memory-that-grows-with-length story")],
 trade=[("Activation memory","every saved tensor lives until backward consumes it — batch × sequence × depth of them"),("Reverse mode pays off for scalar outputs","one output, many inputs is exactly training; forward mode would need one sweep per parameter"),("Gradients accumulate","which is why you call zero_grad(), and how a tensor used twice gets the sum of both paths")],
 say="Each node knows only its local derivative. Reverse mode multiplies them from the loss backwards, so one sweep gives every parameter's gradient."),

dict(id="sgd", g=12, title="SGD and mini-batches", tag="the gradient you can afford",
 intro="The exact gradient is an average over the whole dataset, which no one can afford per step. SGD takes the average over a handful of examples instead: a noisy estimate, thirty times cheaper, and the noise turns out to be a feature. Every large model is trained this way.",
 problem="One full-batch gradient step costs a pass over the data. On real datasets that is hours per step, and most of the pass is spent confirming what the first few hundred examples already said.",
 solves="A mini-batch gradient is an unbiased estimate of the true one, with noise ∝ 1/√B, at cost B instead of N. Many cheap noisy steps beat one exact step by a mile for the same compute — and the noise seems to steer training toward flatter minima that generalise better.",
 method="Shuffle the data each <b>epoch</b>; take it in <b>batches</b> of B; one <b>step</b> per batch, so N/B steps per epoch. Batch size sets the noise and the cost; the learning rate scales up with the batch (the linear scaling rule) up to a critical size, with warm-up. Because the noise never vanishes, η is decayed over training so the walk settles.",
 rel=[("optimizers","momentum and Adam are built on this gradient"),("backprop","how one example's gradient is computed"),("continuous-batching","the same word, a different meaning in serving"),("model-parallelism","data parallelism = the batch split across GPUs, gradients averaged")],
 trade=[("Noise never fully goes away","the jitter near the minimum is ∝ ησ/√B, which is why schedules exist"),("Batch size is a hardware decision too","a bigger batch is a bigger matmul; GPUs want it large, generalisation sometimes wants it smaller"),("Shuffling matters","un-shuffled batches give correlated, biased gradient estimates")],
 say="SGD is gradient descent with a cheap, noisy gradient. B sets the noise, η scales with B, and the noise is why learning-rate schedules exist."),

dict(id="optimizers", g=12, title="Optimizers", tag="from a gradient to an update — GD, SGD, momentum, Adam",
 intro="The gradient says which way is downhill; the optimizer decides how far to step, and in which direction once momentum and scaling get involved. Most of what makes large-model training work or fail lives here, in one hyperparameter and two running averages.",
 problem="Plain gradient descent follows the raw gradient with one learning rate for every parameter. On a surface that is steep one way and flat another — which is what real loss surfaces look like — it zigzags across the steep direction and crawls along the flat one, and a learning rate that is safe for one is wrong for the other.",
 solves="Momentum averages out the zigzag. Adam normalises each parameter's step by the typical size of its own gradient, so steep and flat directions move at the same pace. A schedule changes the learning rate over time so early steps are safe and late ones settle.",
 method="<b>GD:</b> w ← w − η∇L. <b>SGD:</b> the same with a mini-batch gradient. <b>Momentum:</b> v ← βv + ∇L, w ← w − ηv. <b>Adam:</b> running means of the gradient (m) and of its square (v); step = η·m̂/√v̂. <b>AdamW:</b> weight decay added as its own term. Warm η up from zero, then decay it.",
 rel=[("sgd","the noisy gradient every optimizer here consumes"),("backprop","where the gradient comes from"),("autodiff",".grad is what optimizer.step() reads"),("forward-pass","the loss being descended"),("model-parallelism","optimizer state is what sharded training has to split")],
 trade=[("η is the hyperparameter","too large diverges, too small stalls — tune it first"),("Adam keeps two extra copies of every parameter","m and v: optimizer state is 2× the model, the largest piece of training memory"),("Noise is a feature","mini-batch noise is what makes large-scale training affordable, and it seems to help generalisation")],
 say="GD is the idea, SGD is what you can afford, momentum fixes the zigzag, Adam fixes the scale, and AdamW with warm-up and cosine decay is the default."),
]
